JRBrown wrote:
> 
> Scarcity may be good for the soul and the playing skills, but
> abundance is more *fun*.

Obviously that depends. 15 years ago I spent a random wish to get
the gauntlets of power, subsequently I thought my wishes should go
for three blessed potions of gain ability, then I tried wishing for
many potions of gain level, and at some time I wished for and wrote
dozens of blessed scrolls of genocide. Each of those decisions was
motivated by the experience that I was lacking something essential
in a previous game; strength, overall stats, HP's and Pw, too many
foes, in the above example. None of those games lead me very far,
you know why, because it was just focussed on a single aspect of
the game, and abundance of any single means to compensate a single
factor was the way I tried to go. Nethack didn't have all of the
above easily available in a single game. Farming is different.

Knowing your options in a game, analyzing the situations correctly,
extrapolating your needs and taking actions to maximize your chance
to get what you need and survive; that's the experience of challenge
and fun that I made.

In wizmode I would have everything in abundance, easily available.
I never had much fun playing wizmode, though, done that only to
explore the game. But it's still "better" (less boring) to play in
wizmode than do farming, IMO.

Others may have a different feeling/experience about how to have fun
with Nethack. Enjoy!

> When you find a wooden harp, do you run
> around charming all the nymphs and snickering sadistically as they get
> slaughtered by the first inventoryless monster they bump into?

Harps and many of the other music instruments are so rare in Nethack,
sadly, that I rarely use any music instrument to influence any monster.
Usually I use blessed scrolls to charm monsters (and recently I read
them while confused), or if I am lucky to have the highly unbalanced
spellbook of charming I use that if possible.

> Is it
> possible to get entirely out of Gehewatsis with the Amulet in 35 moves
> with 35 cursed potions of gain level?

Potions of gain level are rare (and can't be synthesized by alchemy,
AFAIK). If I would have everything in abundance it would really be no
fun for me. (OTOH, you can hear me cursing if the game does not provide
even a single specific item that I need, all through the dungeons from
level 1 to the VS; but that's Nethack, try to solve your problems _and_
*manage* your resources in advance, so that you have options later.

If you have fun just applying heaps of farmed items... your choice.

> Don't you want a huge stack of
> charm monster scrolls and a magic whistle so you can get one of every
> possible type of pet?

Incidentally it is possible in Nethack to get (at least theoretically)
up to 120 pets with a single scroll; no abundance of scrolls necessary.
That design decision is a bit strange, I think, but it's limited by the
amount of pets you can handle, anyway, so not much of a balance issue.

> Or is this the sort of thing that loses its
> attraction once you've ascended a few times?

For the first (few) ascension(s) I had been happy if I got a full wand
of wishing, or if I found one of my bones levels with a couple more
items than I'd have in a pure standalone game. The focus was indeed to
ascend by any means. (Though at that times (NH 3.0.9) there hadn't been
pudding farming in Nethack - or at least I didn't knew about it.)

It becomes indeed less relevant to have an abundance of items once your
proficiency increases (and you need not necessarily ascend to make that
experience).

Fixing a design flaw is of course a different discussion than speaking
of the fun exploiting a flaw.

